WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:13.060
A detection program manages detections through a defined lifecycle: intake, design, data assessment, development, review, validation, deployment, observation, tuning, maintenance, and retirement.

2
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:20.680
Lifecycle stages make quality repeatable and prevent unsupported queries from becoming permanent operational dependencies.

3
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.960
Each detection needs an owner and durable documentation.

4
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:39.780
Record the use case, risk, logic, data sources, required fields, asset scope, assumptions, exclusions, severity, investigation steps, safe response guidance, test evidence, and review date.

5
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.800
Version control preserves history, peer review, and rollback.

6
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.520
Metrics should describe usefulness, not just volume.

7
00:00:47.620 --> 00:01:00.260
Track data-source health, coverage of critical assets and use cases, false-positive disposition, time to triage, missing enrichment, repeated incidents, and analyst or operator feedback.

8
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.840
A high alert count may indicate noise rather than effective detection.

9
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:14.880
Retire detections when the risk disappears, architecture changes, a source is decommissioned, logic is superseded, or the signal no longer supports action.

10
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:21.620
Retirement should update coverage records and preserve history so a control gap is not created silently.

11
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:28.860
SOC integration requires consistent fields, routing, case management, runbooks, and escalation contacts.

12
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:41.440
OT alerts should include asset role, site, criticality, protocol or activity, maintenance context, and the operations or engineering team able to validate process impact.

13
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:50.760
Continuous improvement comes from investigations, incidents, change records, threat information, telemetry failures, and exercises.

14
00:01:50.860 --> 00:02:02.940
Security, operations, and engineering share responsibility: detection engineers maintain logic, analysts investigate evidence, and process experts guide interpretation and safe action.

15
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.440
Together they make the program trustworthy.
